{"version":1,"slug":"neural-networks","topic":"How does a neural network learn?","level":"intermediate","title":"How a Neural Network Learns","summary":"By the end of this lesson, you will understand how a neural network makes predictions, measures its mistakes, and adjusts its weights step by step to improve.","scopeNote":null,"scenes":[{"id":"intro","kind":"intro","title":"How does a neural network learn?","segments":[{"id":"intro-0","narration":"How can a computer learn to recognize a handwritten seven without anyone ever writing a single rule for what a seven looks like?","sig":"XgIfxOoDFt-98ynqS2-nAYzk5Tl04rCi","actions":[{"id":"hero","kind":"icon","cue":"How can a","text":"Neural Network","items":null,"icon":"brain","at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":4,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"intro-1","narration":"Here's our plan. First, Making a Guess. Then, Learning from Mistakes. And finally, A Worked Example.","sig":"jvWEwFlyfM8kKASjhAB2Vu9KfE9Pq2kY","actions":[{"id":"agenda","kind":"list","cue":"Here's our plan","text":null,"items":["Making a Guess","Learning from Mistakes","A Worked Example"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":1,"w":7,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c1","kind":"chapter","title":"Making a Guess","segments":[{"id":"c1-0","narration":"At the heart of a neural network is a single artificial neuron. It takes in numbers as inputs, like hours studied or practice tests taken.","sig":"C9VOv9jPROK8fqMe_nFAlA6_FomFiQae","actions":[{"id":"neuron","kind":"circle","cue":"single artificial neuron","text":"Neuron","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"in1","kind":"box","cue":"hours studied","text":"Hours studied","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":2,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"in2","kind":"box","cue":"practice tests taken","text":"Practice tests","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":1,"row":4,"w":2,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-1","narration":"Each input is multiplied by a weight, which measures how important that input is. Big weights mean that input matters more.","sig":"OF4uw5BYiSp0aHHB3uKAEmtCoym-nJBn","actions":[{"id":"w1","kind":"arrow","cue":"multiplied by a weight","text":"× weight 1","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"in1","to":"neuron","target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"w2","kind":"arrow","cue":"multiplied by a weight","text":"× weight 2","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"in2","to":"neuron","target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"t1","kind":"trace","cue":"input matters more","text":"value","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"w1","color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-2","narration":"The neuron adds all those weighted inputs together and passes the total forward to make a prediction.","sig":"Wzcu9xPcPnBWxIHi1k7KtTkh60AFRl2J","actions":[{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"adds all those","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"neuron","color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"pred","kind":"box","cue":"passes the total","text":"Prediction","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":2,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"out_arr","kind":"arrow","cue":"make a prediction","text":"sum","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"neuron","to":"pred","target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"t2","kind":"trace","cue":"make a prediction","text":"guess","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"out_arr","color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-3","narration":"Right now, it is just a guess. Next, we will see how the network learns from its mistakes.","sig":"PFgjvmA15t-aAN-Na96-3I1BiZt0A2eL","actions":[{"id":"hl","kind":"highlight","cue":"just a guess","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"pred","color":null,"expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c2","kind":"chapter","title":"Learning from Mistakes","segments":[{"id":"c2-0","narration":"Making a guess is only the start, because a network also needs to learn from its mistakes. It does not memorize answers like a database; instead, it adjusts its numerical weights to capture patterns.","sig":"ErfTCDokZ98iUFXtfwvpEXRnwCBZqA0S","actions":[{"id":"wrong","kind":"box","cue":"memorize answers","text":"Memorizes answers","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":0,"w":3,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"red","expr":null},{"id":"x1","kind":"cross_out","cue":"not memorize answers","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"wrong","color":"red","expr":null},{"id":"right","kind":"box","cue":"adjusts its numerical","text":"Adjusts weights","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":3,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"c1","kind":"check","cue":"capture patterns","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"right","color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-1","narration":"This happens in a four-step loop. First it predicts, then it measures the error with a loss function, sends that error backward through backpropagation, and updates the weights.","sig":"9ieoH2JB2BVNQgB-0qk3BZDTDyOtqFsO","actions":[{"id":"loop","kind":"cycle","cue":"four-step loop","text":null,"items":["Predict","Measure Loss","Backpropagate","Update Weights"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":2,"w":6,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"tr1","kind":"trace","cue":"sends that error","text":null,"items":null,"icon":"refresh-cw","at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"loop","color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-2","narration":"To know which way to adjust, it uses gradient descent. Imagine a U-shaped loss curve: the network steps downhill until it reaches the lowest error at the bottom.","sig":"0HN2FHcrkWtY1_3dvJt4OZl4TSN8h1TF","actions":[{"id":"curve","kind":"plot","cue":"U-shaped loss curve","text":"Loss Curve","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":7,"row":1,"w":5,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"purple","expr":"x^2; -3..3"},{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"steps downhill","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"curve","color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"min_note","kind":"callout","cue":"lowest error","text":"Lowest error","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"curve","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c3","kind":"chapter","title":"A Worked Example","segments":[{"id":"c3-0","narration":"Now let us put that learning loop into action with real numbers. Suppose a neuron takes an input of three hours studied and multiplies it by an initial weight of ten.","sig":"WLt7GncrrKbmJYQh4d3f9ZBvE49OZGYM","actions":[{"id":"inp","kind":"box","cue":"three hours studied","text":"Input: 3 hours","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"wt","kind":"box","cue":"initial weight of ten","text":"Weight: 10","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":1,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"purple","expr":null},{"id":"a1","kind":"arrow","cue":"multiplies it by","text":"×","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"inp","to":"wt","target":null,"color":null,"expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-1","narration":"Three times ten gives a prediction of thirty, but the true exam score is forty five. That means our prediction is fifteen points too low.","sig":"pIMpfCnKRlJYiwc2SkHnBWsJY8PqKzoC","actions":[{"id":"pred","kind":"box","cue":"prediction of thirty","text":"Prediction: 30","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":8,"row":1,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"a2","kind":"arrow","cue":"prediction of thirty","text":"=","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"wt","to":"pred","target":null,"color":null,"expr":null},{"id":"true_score","kind":"box","cue":"true exam score","text":"True Score: 45","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":8,"row":4,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"err_callout","kind":"callout","cue":"fifteen points too low","text":"15 points too low","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"pred","color":"red","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-2","narration":"Gradient descent tells the neuron to nudge its weight upward. If we bump the weight up to twelve, three times twelve gives thirty six, moving us closer to the truth.","sig":"Ujzws8A6I_anReHk-TDQAPKbovYkmfqJ","actions":[{"id":"a3","kind":"arrow","cue":"nudge its weight upward","text":"nudge up","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"wt","to":"new_wt","target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"new_wt","kind":"number","cue":"weight up to twelve","text":"12","items":["Updated Weight"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":4,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"closer to the truth","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"new_wt","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"Recap","segments":[{"id":"recap-0","narration":"So remember: neurons weigh inputs to make a guess, learn by nudging weights down the loss curve, and get closer with every step. What questions do you have about how neural networks learn?","sig":"P_-aBuaECM296D5r7_5DmjsGOPzo05j8","actions":[{"id":"recap_list","kind":"list","cue":"neurons weigh inputs","text":null,"items":["Neurons weigh inputs to guess","Loss and gradient descent guide updates","Weights nudge closer each step"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":6,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"recap_icon","kind":"icon","cue":"What questions do you","text":"Ask anything","items":null,"icon":"brain","at":{"col":8,"row":2,"w":3,"h":3},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]}],"quiz":[{"question":"What does a higher weight on an input tell the artificial neuron?","options":["That input is less important","That input matters more","The neuron should ignore it","The final answer is memorized"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A larger weight multiplies the input by a bigger number, giving it more influence over the neuron's prediction.","chapter":1},{"question":"How does gradient descent help a neural network learn?","options":["It stores every past answer","It deletes wrong inputs","It steps weights downhill toward lower error","It guesses random numbers forever"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Gradient descent adjusts the weights in the direction that moves the loss function downhill toward the lowest error.","chapter":2},{"question":"In the worked example, why did the neuron increase its weight from ten to twelve?","options":["Its prediction of thirty was too low","Its prediction of thirty was too high","The input hours decreased to zero","The true score was twenty"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because thirty was fifteen points below the true score of forty-five, increasing the weight moved the prediction closer to the truth.","chapter":3}],"suggestions":["How activation functions work","Deep neural networks and layers","Overfitting and training data"],"models":{"lesson":"google/gemini-4-hydrogen","voice":"google/gemini-3.8-flash-tts"},"createdAt":"2026-11-17T12:47:29.190Z","audioBase":"/samples/neural-networks"}